{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe canonical bridge surface should expose only conventional process statuses:\n\n- 0: the requested bridge operation completed or reached a benign no-work/yield/paused state.\n- 1: operational failure requiring attention.\n- 2: invalid invocation or configuration.\n\nToday the legacy reconciler process also emits 3 (another pass is active), 4 (legacy phase gate),\nand 75 (reschedule). The current workflow copies a whitelist and the library copies a different\none, which has already produced divergent behavior.\n\nThis ticket moves state classification into a typed core disposition and makes the new\n`rebar bridge preview` / `rebar bridge sync` surface map that disposition to 0/1/2. It does not\nbreak environments that still use the old vocabulary. Legacy `rebar reconcile --mode ...` and\ndirect `python -m rebar_reconciler --mode ...` compatibility routes retain their historical\n3/4/75 translations, messages, and defaults. New and old routes share one core result; only their\nouter compatibility adapters translate it differently.\n\nThat split is required for rolling deployment: current workflow revisions migrate to the canonical\nbridge command and can delete their numeric whitelist, while an older checked-out workflow\ncontinues to receive the codes it already understands. Any future retirement of legacy codes is a\nseparate deprecation decision after legacy entrypoints are no longer supported.\n\n`1104-93eb-6ce0-4a19` lands first because it defines paused as a benign typed state and preserves\nlegacy gate behavior for this story to translate. `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51` retains the old command\nand engine flags as compatibility entrypoints.\n\n## Core disposition\n\nIntroduce or reuse a small typed result/disposition at the reconciler boundary that distinguishes:\n\n- converged/success;\n- paused;\n- another live pass in flight;\n- reschedule requested;\n- legacy phase gate blocked;\n- invalid invocation/configuration;\n- operational failure.\n\nThe core owns messages and semantic classification, not process-specific numeric whitelists.\nCanonical bridge adapters map the first five benign dispositions to rc 0 with one stable,\nsingle-line explanation naming the state; invalid input/configuration maps to 2; operational failure\nmaps to 1.\n\nLegacy CLI/direct-engine adapters map the same dispositions to the existing 0/3/4/75/1/2 contract.\nThey do not rerun or reclassify the operation. This retains behavior for systemd, Jenkins,\nself-hosted actions, shell scripts, and older workflow revisions while allowing the new surface to\nbe simple.\n\nThe canonical library/MCP bridge adapters consume the typed disposition directly. Legacy\n`rebar.reconcile` and MCP `reconcile` compatibility behavior is retained by `f789`; this story\nprovides the shared semantic result they consume.\n\n## Workflow and deployment migration\n\nMigrate `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` to the canonical bridge invocation and delete\nits `case` whitelist. The workflow treats rc 0 as benign/success, rc 1 as operational failure,\nand rc 2 as invocation/configuration failure; state-specific operator text comes from core.\nThe exact paused marker/commit-skip contract from `1104` remains intact.\n\nBefore changing canonical output, search deployment units and infrastructure for consumers of 3,\n4, or 75, including systemd `RestartForceExitStatus=75`, Jenkins shells, workflow conditions, and\nprobe scripts. Every in-repo consumer migrates in this change or is explicitly retained as a legacy\ncompatibility consumer. The retained legacy translation guarantees out-of-repo consumers are not\nbroken during the vocabulary migration.\n\nDocument both contracts: canonical bridge is 0/1/2; legacy reconcile compatibility retains its\nhistorical codes for continuity.\n\n## Test plan\n\nUse real subprocess entrypoints over deterministic temporary repositories. Seed each disposition\nand exercise both the canonical bridge command and the legacy command/direct-engine route:\n\n- converged;\n- paused;\n- another live pass;\n- reschedule;\n- legacy phase gate;\n- invalid arguments/config;\n- hard failure.\n\nFor each row, assert exact exit status, one-line state explanation, and identical repository/ref\npostconditions across old and new routes. Canonical benign rows must all be 0; legacy rows must\nretain 3/4/75 where historically applicable. Prove no adapter double-runs a pass and no benign\ntranslation mutates state.\n\nParse and execute the production workflow block to prove the numeric whitelist is absent, canonical\n0/1/2 behavior is sufficient, the exact paused marker still skips commit-and-push, and rc 1/2 fail.\nRetain a fixture representing the old workflow/legacy command pairing and prove it still sees its\nhistorical codes.\n\nSearch the repository and deployment manifests for numeric consumers. The test fails on an\nunclassified primary-surface consumer while allowing explicit compatibility tests/docs and the\nlegacy translation table.\n\nSurface parity census:\n\n- CLI: in scope through canonical bridge and legacy reconcile subprocesses.\n- Direct engine: in scope through primary subcommands and legacy `--mode`.\n- Library: shared-disposition contract in scope; public adapter behavior completes in `f789`.\n- MCP: shared-disposition contract in scope; public adapter behavior completes in `f789`.\n- Automated callers: in scope through current and legacy workflow fixtures, deployment-unit search,\n  and probe/script inventory.\n- Dual direction: in scope; preview/non-mutation and sync/mutation share dispositions without\n  changing state semantics.\n\nNo new contention harness is required: the change classifies results after existing lock/CAS seams\nand adds no retry, cache, or composed write.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Canonical `bridge preview` / `bridge sync` subprocesses emit only 0, 1, or 2; every benign converged, paused, in-flight, reschedule, or legacy-gated outcome exits 0 with its stable one-line state explanation; no canonical adapter emits 3, 4, or 75 (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:52-87`, canonical held-out subprocess matrix).\n- [x] Legacy `rebar reconcile --mode ...` and direct engine `--mode` routes retain historical 3/4/75 translations, messages, and defaults, and no old-vocabulary workflow/environment is forced onto canonical numeric behavior (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:52-87`, `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_lock_steal.py`, legacy held-out subprocess matrix).\n- [x] Canonical and legacy adapters consume one typed core disposition, run a pass once, and produce identical repository/ref effects; library/MCP core consumers use that shared disposition boundary while sibling `f789` owns their public new/compatibility adapters (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py`, `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py`, `tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_cli_heldout.py`).\n- [x] `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` uses the canonical bridge surface with only 0/1/2 and preserves exact paused commit-skip behavior, while the legacy workflow/command fixture stays green for every historical benign code (`tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py:181-193`, `tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_cli_heldout.py`).\n- [x] Deployment units, Jenkins/GitHub workflows, scripts, and probes have been searched for 3/4/75 consumers and every in-tree site is migrated or classified compatibility; `docs/exit-codes.md:29-54` and `docs/release-notes.md:29-46` document canonical 0/1/2, retained translations, and rolling-migration continuity.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py`: typed semantic disposition and route-specific\n  canonical versus legacy process translation.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py`: consume shared disposition without a divergent canonical whitelist;\n  preserve legacy adapter behavior until `f789` finalizes the public surface.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`: canonical invocation and 0/1/2 handling with paused\n  commit-skip.\n- focused reconciler/CLI/library tests: old/new subprocess disposition matrix and state invariants.\n- workflow contract tests: canonical simplified block plus legacy-workflow continuity fixture.\n- deployment/config search tests: classify every numeric consumer.\n- `docs/exit-codes.md` and `docs/release-notes.md`: dual contract and migration guidance.\n\n## Completion proof map\n\n- c07 numeric-consumer inventory: `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:25-29` is the canonical production contract; `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py:181-193` executes that run block for 0/1/2 and fails if the old numeric case whitelist exists. A tracked-source search across `.github`, `scripts`, and `infra` for bridge/reconciler references within 80 characters of 3/4/75 has no production sentinel handler; compatibility occurrences are confined to the legacy adapter/tests/docs.\n- c08 documentation: `docs/exit-codes.md:29-54` documents canonical 0/1/2, all benign state markers, retained 3/4/75 translations, and rolling migration. `docs/release-notes.md:29-46` records the same dual contract and continuity for systemd/Jenkins/scripts/checked-out workflows.\n- c09 canonical constraint and legacy continuity: `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:52-66` defines one `_Disposition` with canonical exits only in {0,1,2} and separate legacy exits 3/4/75; `:68-87` returns `canonical_exit` only for preview/sync and `legacy_exit` otherwise. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_main.py`, `tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_cli_heldout.py`, and `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_lock_steal.py` exercise both boundaries.\n\n## Canonical message contract\n\nThe core owns these exact benign canonical stderr lines so bridge adapters never fall back to legacy-route wording:\n\n- converged: `BRIDGE_STATE: converged`\n- paused: `BRIDGE_PAUSED: {…}` (the existing single-line JSON marker)\n- another pass in flight: `BRIDGE_STATE: in-flight`\n- reschedule requested: `BRIDGE_STATE: reschedule`\n- historical phase gate: `BRIDGE_STATE: legacy-gated`\n\nThe first acceptance criterion and the subprocess matrix assert these exact lines. Legacy compatibility routes keep their established messages unchanged."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786289714731003001,"uuid":"66893525-18b4-49dd-8f3b-89c50be1ba60"}